{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789481856,"nonce":"i0pcFeryIGMInH8xG7WLo6sxixE=","title":"The inliner unrolls a recursive callee one level per round into any caller outside its cycle","message":"The inliner refuses a callee in the caller's own cycle, but a callee that re-enters its cycle from a caller outside it comes back as the call its body carries, and the next round inlines that too. In the Python builtins snapshot, `zb_any_hash` recurses on tuples and `zb_any_eq` on lists, so `zb_dict_reindex` and `zb_dict_add_pairs` (two short loops in the DSL) came out at about 1600 lines each, holding seven copies of the hash. Every user function that calls a recursive function in a loop got the same treatment up to the caller budget.\n\nCost, on the M1 Pro: those two functions took 9.4 and 7.2 ms to compile on first call (first-call compiles count in run time), and bench_tokenize's run held 25 ms of such compiles. The module compile of the data kernels was 55 to 60 ms.\n\nFix: `call_cycles` also reports which components are re-entered by a call from within them (a self-call included), and `inline_in_function` refuses such a callee anywhere. The recursive inliner still peels direct self-calls on its own terms. After: first-call compiles on bench_tokenize 25 to 11 ms, on bench_records 25 to 9 ms; module compile of bench_tokenize 60 to 32 ms; exec unchanged or better (bench_records 215 to 200 ms with the smaller library).\n\nNot done: peeling one level of a recursive callee into a hot caller, which needs a provenance mark on the calls an inlined body brings in so the next round does not expand them again.","files":null}]}